@page "/getting-started"

<PageTitle>Getting started · Bdnd</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        One package and one service registration - then a <code>BdndProvider</code> around any interactively
        rendered region, with draggables and drop targets inside it. You drag <em>data</em> of a <em>type</em>,
        not a view, and the drag state lives in C#.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        In a Blazor Web App with an interactive client, the package belongs in the <code>.Client</code> project,
        next to the pages that drag.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">dotnet add package Bit.Bdnd</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Register the service</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        It registers <code>BdndInterop</code>, the scoped wrapper around the script module that every draggable and
        drop target injects. The drag-and-drop manager itself is not a service: each <code>BdndProvider</code>
        creates its own and shares it with the components inside it. In a Blazor Web App, register it in both
        <code>Program.cs</code> files - the pages are prerendered on the server before they run in the browser.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@RegisterCode" />
    <p class="bb-card-desc">And the namespace, in <code>_Imports.razor</code>:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ImportsCode" />
    <div class="bb-note">
        There is no stylesheet to link and no script tag to add: the components import their module,
        <code>_content/Bit.Bdnd/bit-bdnd.js</code>, themselves the first time they need it.
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> A first drag and drop</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Wrap the region in a <code>BdndProvider</code> and declare sources and targets inside it. A draggable's
        <code>Type</code> is what a target matches against its <code>Accept</code> list, and its <code>Item</code>
        is what the target's <code>OnDrop</code> receives. Each one's child content gets the collected state -
        <code>IsDragging</code> for a source, <code>IsOver</code> and <code>CanDrop</code> for a target - to
        render from.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span></div>
        <FirstDrop />

        <p class="demo-caption">
            Drag a box with the mouse, press and hold one with a finger, or <kbd>Tab</kbd> to it and press
            <kbd>Space</kbd>, an arrow key and <kbd>Space</kbd> again: the same markup takes all three.
        </p>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock Code="@FirstDropCode" />

    <div class="bb-note">
        Three things make this work: the components sit inside one <code>BdndProvider</code>; the draggable's
        <code>Type</code> is one of the types the target lists in <code>Accept</code>; and the page is interactive,
        because a statically rendered page never wires up drag events.
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Core concepts</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Bdnd brings React DnD's data-driven model to Blazor as components rather than hooks.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Concept</th><th>In Bdnd</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Items and types</td>
                    <td>You drag an item - any C# object - of a type, a string. A target accepts the types it lists in <code>Accept</code> or <code>AcceptTypes</code>; the item's .NET type does not take part.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Components</td>
                    <td><code>BdndProvider</code>, <code>BdndDraggable</code>, <code>BdndDropTarget</code>, <code>BdndDragLayer</code> and <code>BdndDragHandle</code> - React DnD's provider, <code>useDrag</code>, <code>useDrop</code>, <code>useDragLayer</code> and a drag handle.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Collected state</td>
                    <td>What a component's child content renders from: <code>IsDragging</code>, <code>IsOver</code>, <code>IsOverShallow</code>, <code>CanDrop</code>, the item and the offsets.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Monitor</td>
                    <td><code>IBdndMonitor</code>, a read-only view of the live drag state, on every collected state and callback argument as <code>Monitor</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Callbacks</td>
                    <td><code>OnBegin</code> and <code>OnEnd</code> on a source; <code>OnHover</code>, <code>OnEnter</code>, <code>OnLeave</code> and <code>OnDrop</code> on a target; <code>OnDragStart</code> and <code>OnDragEnd</code> on the provider for every drag. A target can publish a result from <code>OnDrop</code>, which the source's <code>OnEnd</code> receives with <code>DidDrop</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Input</td>
                    <td>The browser's own HTML5 drag and drop for the mouse - so files, text and links dragged in from outside the browser drop too - pointer events for touch and pen, and the keyboard (<kbd>Space</kbd>, the arrow keys, <kbd>Space</kbd>) for everyone. Every input runs the same callbacks.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Styling hooks</td>
                    <td>Hosts carry <code>data-bit-bdnd-dragging</code>, <code>data-bit-bdnd-can-drop</code>, <code>data-bit-bdnd-over</code>, <code>data-bit-bdnd-over-shallow</code> and <code>data-bit-bdnd-edge</code> while a drag is in progress, so a stylesheet can follow it. Bdnd ships no stylesheet of its own.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Where to go next</h3>
